{
  "query": "At a travel booking startup named AeroVista you are defining IAM access for Google Cloud SQL. One teammate must be able to create, configure and delete Cloud SQL instances and manage maintenance and backups, but they must not be able to read or change any rows inside the databases. Which IAM role should you assign to this user?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "roles/cloudsql.viewer",
      "explanation": "This role allows a user to view Cloud SQL instance configurations and metadata without making changes",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "roles/cloudsql.client",
      "explanation": "This role allows a user to connect to Cloud SQL instances and use the client connector for access",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "roles/cloudsql.admin",
      "explanation": "This role permits comprehensive management of Cloud SQL resources including creating and modifying instances and settings",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "roles/cloudsql.instanceUser",
      "explanation": "This role lets a user authenticate to the database using IAM database authentication mapped to a database user",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>roles/cloudsql.admin</b> because it allows full management of Cloud SQL instances including creating, configuring, deleting, and handling maintenance and backups while not granting the ability to read or modify data rows inside the databases.</p><p>This role is designed for administrative control of Cloud SQL resources. It covers instance lifecycle operations and configuration tasks such as maintenance windows, flags, backups, and failover settings. Access to the data inside the databases still requires database user credentials and corresponding database privileges, which are separate from IAM permissions, so this role meets the requirement to avoid row level access.</p><p><i>roles/cloudsql.viewer</i> is read only for Cloud SQL resources and can only view instance metadata and configuration. It cannot create, modify, or delete instances and it cannot manage maintenance or backups, so it does not meet the management requirement.</p><p><i>roles/cloudsql.client</i> enables connecting to Cloud SQL instances using the Auth Proxy or connectors. It does not provide permissions to create, configure, or delete instances, so it fails the management requirement and focuses on connectivity rather than administration.</p><p><i>roles/cloudsql.instanceUser</i> is used with IAM database authentication to log in to a database as a mapped user. It is oriented toward data access and does not grant instance administration, which makes it unsuitable for the stated need.</p>",
  "batch_id": "22",
